</asy>
<imath>\textbf{(A)} ~\dfrac{1}{16}\qquad\textbf{(B)} ~\dfrac{1}{8}\qquad\textbf{(C)} ~\dfrac{1}{4}\qquad\textbf{(D)} ~\dfrac{3}{8}\qquad\textbf{(E)} ~\dfrac{1}{2}</imath>

==Solution 1==

First, we calculate that there are a total of <imath>4\cdot4=16</imath> possibilities. Now, we list all of two-digit perfect squares. <imath>64</imath> and <imath>81</imath> are the only ones that can be made using the spinner. Consequently, there is a <imath>\frac{2}{16}=\boxed{\textbf{(B) }\dfrac{1}{8}}</imath> probability that the number formed by the two spinners is a perfect square.

~MathFun1000

==Solution 2==

There are <imath>4 \cdot 4 = 16</imath> total possibilities of <imath>N</imath>. We know <imath>N=10A+B</imath>, which <imath>A</imath> is a number from spinner <imath>A</imath>, and <imath>B</imath> is a number from spinner <imath>B</imath>. Also, notice that there are no perfect squares in the <imath>50</imath>s or <imath>70</imath>s, so only  <imath>4-2=2</imath> values of N work, namely <imath>64</imath> and <imath>81</imath>. Hence, <imath>\frac{2}{16}=\boxed{\textbf{(B) }\dfrac{1}{8}}</imath>.

~MrThinker

==Solution 3==
 We can see that the smallest number on the spinner 1 is 5. Possibilities for 5 are 51,52,53, and 54
Second one is 6 in which we get 61,62,63,64. We see that 64 is a square number.
The next number is 7. Possibilities are 71,72,73, and 74. None of them are squares.
Finally, we have 8. 81,82,83, and 84 are the numbers possible. 81 is a perfect square.
As our trusty probability formula tells us, probability is the number of desired outcomes over possible outcomes.
The possible outcomes are 16 and the desired outcomes are 2. The fraction is 2/16. In simplest terms, it is 1/8.
Bringing to answer choice (B)
